   "stem": {
      "text": "A curve is given in polar coordinates by \\( r = 2 - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\), where \\( x = r\\cos\\theta \\) and \\( y = r\\sin\\theta \\). At a point P on the curve, \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{4}{11} + \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{11} \\) and \\( \\frac{dy}{d\\theta} = -2 - \\sqrt{3} \\). A calculator may be used. Without differentiating the polar equation, find the exact value of \\( \\frac{dx}{d\\theta} \\) at P, and show the work that leads to your answer.",
